**Read:** [Solid State Physics — Steven H. Simon, 2012 draft](../sources/Solid-State-Physics-Simon-2012.lingua#r_3R4Q71S31MD0EH6YVDQQ2ESKH5), chapters 2–4 and 8–9; [Statistical Physics — David Tong](../sources/Statistical-Physics-Tong.lingua#r_7FMG47HD1MT706NT3CABRTFNS2), chapter 3; [Nano-to-Macro Transport Processes — MIT 2.57](../../../physics/thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/sources/Nano-to-Macro-Transport-MIT-2-57.lingua#r_1YF7TD689ZRGQ51D4B3NGVHZQG), microscopic energy and carrier statistics.

An oscillator contributes C/kB=x² exp(x)/(exp(x)−1)², with x=ℏω/kBT. Summing over a solid's mode spectrum differs from assigning one frequency to every vibration. Explain how a three-dimensional acoustic spectrum gives the Debye low-temperature T³ law and how a simple degenerate metal adds an electronic term proportional to T. These are regimes, not universal fits over every temperature.

At x=1 the oscillator contributes about 0.920674 kB. Its energy includes a temperature-independent zero-point term, which contributes no heat capacity. Keep C_V distinct from C_p; in a homogeneous isotropic equilibrium solid C_p−C_V=T V α_V² K_T with consistent total or molar quantities.

**Work:** Derive the limits, fit a documented heat-capacity dataset with stated normalization, and examine residuals before adding mechanisms. Distinguish an impurity transition or magnetic contribution from a change in the fitted Debye temperature.

